<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vendôme Column</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1810</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Colonne Vendôme</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vendôme Column is a monumental column in the Place Vendôme in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, erected in 1806–1810 on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s orders to commemorate the victory at Austerlitz. Modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajans-column">Trajan's Column</a>, it is sheathed in bronze reliefs said to be cast from captured enemy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannon</a> and topped by a statue of Napoleon. The Paris Commune pulled it down in May 1871 as a symbol of militarism, and it was re-erected in 1875.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vendome-column.jpg" alt="Vendôme Column" width="320" height="110" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giorgio Galeotti · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Place_Vendome,_Paris_20_April_2011.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
